+**4.2.1 Minimum verification actions of the pre-reviewer (five items)**:
+
+1. For artifacts mentioned but not expanded in the diff (harness scripts, test files, evidence artifacts, report attachments, etc.), the key files themselves must actually be read; a conclusion must not rest on the report narrative or speculation alone.
+2. For key numbers, timestamps and state-sequence assertions involved in the diff or the report, at least 2 must be independently recomputed: re-derive when raw artifacts are involved; locate the reference point and check it when cross-file references are involved.
+3. Any contradiction (however small) found between the narrative of the report or design document and the evidence artifacts must be listed as an independent finding with a severity - it must not be let go as "probably just a typo".
+4. The counterexample analysis must give at least one falsifiable criterion of the form "if guard X is deleted / logic Y is reverted, which assertion or piece of evidence would fail"; if there is none, the reason must be stated explicitly.
+5. The conclusion must rest on verification the reviewer actually performed; the report's own self-description must not be cited as evidence (self-attestation is not trustworthy).
+
+The above are minimum verification actions; they must not replace independent judgment; going beyond the minimum to dig deeper is likewise not restricted.
+
+**4.2.2 Prohibited items of the pre-reviewer (authoritative wording)**: editing files; using test code or documentation changes themselves as the basis for judging implementation correctness (reading the above artifacts to verify report claims is not restricted by this; keep an independent perspective).
+
 ### 4.3 Severity Definitions
 
 | Level | Meaning | Handling |
 |---|---|---|
-| Critical | data loss, security boundary failure, silently wrong conclusion | must fix and re-run that review stage |
-| High | functional failure, crash, contract conflict | same as above |
+| Critical | data loss, security boundary failure, silently wrong conclusion; and a report whose core causation is overturned by its own evidence belongs to this level | must fix and re-run that review stage |
+| High | functional failure, crash, contract conflict; a protocol property that does not match the design but fail-closed already prevented a wrong conclusion belongs to this level | same as above |
 | Medium | latent defect, coverage gap, bilingual mismatch | same as above ("Medium+" is blocking) |
 | Low | style, naming, comments, readability | recorded on the ledger, may be deferred |
 
+> **Severity-boundary note**: the same substantive finding is not weakened by a difference in severity rating - the rating determines the handling action (re-run that stage vs a targeted re-check), not whether the finding stands.
